WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:01.089
Good morning, John.

00:00:01.089 --> 00:00:06.790
Thank you so much for your video on Monday.
It felt like there was this hole, and Nerdfighteria

00:00:06.790 --> 00:00:10.860
had been asking us this question for so long,
and we had left it unanswered, and it felt

00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:16.219
very... it felt kinda wrong. It felt kinda
like a gap in our body of work.

00:00:16.219 --> 00:00:20.699
And it points out that often, asking questions
about the questions that we want to ask is

00:00:20.699 --> 00:00:25.461
far more useful than answering the questions
themselves. So for example, interesting question

00:00:25.461 --> 00:00:28.970
about the phrase "belief in God." There are
more words that are hard to define in that

00:00:28.970 --> 00:00:30.939
sentence than words are easy to define.

00:00:30.939 --> 00:00:36.290
For example, "God"? Even people who go to
the same church, ostensibly believe in the

00:00:36.290 --> 00:00:40.000
same god, all have different visions of what
God is in their head. Everyone is always going

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:45.003
to have a slightly different perception of
what that thing-- he, she, it, is. But at

00:00:45.003 --> 00:00:49.120
the same time, it's a very particular thing
to each particular person.

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:53.140
And then you have the word "belief." Interestingly,
uh, the question of what the difference between

00:00:53.140 --> 00:00:59.399
belief and knowledge is is not just a philosophical
question; it is an entire branch of philosophy

00:00:59.399 --> 00:01:04.059
called epistemology. So obviously, uh, not
something to cover in a video blog, but the

00:01:04.059 --> 00:01:09.479
longest-running definition is that knowledge
is belief that is true. Which is kinda strange

00:01:09.479 --> 00:01:13.560
to get your head around, which is why there
is an entire branch of philosophy, uh, concerning

00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:14.560
it.

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:17.600
When we believe something, we accept that
there is a chance that it's not true, but

00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:21.029
we have faith nonetheless that it is true.
Like it's weird to talk about having faith

00:01:21.029 --> 00:01:26.049
in things that you know, like I know that
this water is not going to poison me if I

00:01:26.049 --> 00:01:29.689
drink it. Whereas, if I say that I have faith
that this water will not kill me if I drink

00:01:29.689 --> 00:01:31.990
it, now I'm not so sure I'm gonna drink it.

00:01:31.990 --> 00:01:35.090
But what really bugs me about the question
"do you believe in God?" is not that there

00:01:35.090 --> 00:01:39.950
are many more interesting questions surrounding
that question, like "why do we ask it?" and

00:01:39.950 --> 00:01:43.530
"what does 'believe' mean?" --is the motivation
behind asking that question. People who have

00:01:43.530 --> 00:01:47.229
asked that question to me, or to other people,
I'm just curious, why do you want to know?

00:01:47.229 --> 00:01:50.069
Because it seems like there's really only
two reasons to want to know the answer to

00:01:50.069 --> 00:01:51.069
that question.

00:01:51.069 --> 00:01:56.559
One: Because you're not sure yourself, and
you're trying to inform your opinion, and

00:01:56.559 --> 00:02:01.049
you think that I'm some kind of authority.
To those people, I say: Thank you for thinking

00:02:01.049 --> 00:02:06.040
that I'm an authority on this. I am not, and
that's going to have to be something that

00:02:06.040 --> 00:02:09.629
you figure out on your own. And if anybody's
telling you different, uh, then they're wrong.

00:02:09.629 --> 00:02:11.650
This is a question for you to answer for yourself.

00:02:11.650 --> 00:02:16.019
There's kind of a 1.5 here, which is that
you want to reinforce your beliefs, and you

00:02:16.019 --> 00:02:20.980
wanna feel like, "Oh Hank, he believes the
same thing as me, so now I feel better about

00:02:20.980 --> 00:02:25.680
my beliefs." Well that's not actually going
to work, uh, for you; you have to feel good

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:27.439
about your beliefs for your own sake.

00:02:27.439 --> 00:02:31.640
So basically 1 and 1.5, I'm not gonna help
you with that, uh, you gotta have to figure

00:02:31.640 --> 00:02:32.790
it out on your own.

00:02:32.790 --> 00:02:39.520
And the second reason to ask if someone is,
believes in God is kind of a shortcut to think

00:02:39.520 --> 00:02:43.709
that you know more about that person because
of a very simple answer, which is dangerous.

00:02:43.709 --> 00:02:49.150
This is the kind of pattern recognition that
we do constantly as people: we try to categorize

00:02:49.150 --> 00:02:54.189
things. And that's usually really useful - until
you start dealing with people.

00:02:54.189 --> 00:02:57.579
Sometimes I feel like when someone asks me
if I believe in God, it's like a blind person

00:02:57.579 --> 00:03:02.530
asking if I'm black so that they can put me
in the right category. People are constantly

00:03:02.530 --> 00:03:07.439
searching for little things that they can
use as markers to identify how and what people

00:03:07.439 --> 00:03:11.180
are. Like, "gay people are all like this,"
"Christians are all like this," "atheists

00:03:11.180 --> 00:03:14.689
are all like this." And that is - I mean,
duh - that's really dangerous!

00:03:14.689 --> 00:03:19.650
So that's my biggest fear, that people are
asking because they want to inform their opinions

00:03:19.650 --> 00:03:25.489
of me with this little one-word answer that
says so very little about who I actually am.

00:03:25.489 --> 00:03:29.890
After you've formed a strong opinion of who
I am and understand how I live my life, that's

00:03:29.890 --> 00:03:33.829
when I'll be comfortable telling you about
my beliefs and practices, which is that I

00:03:33.829 --> 00:03:38.109
am a Satan worshipper who feeds on the blood
of newborn unicorns.

00:03:38.109 --> 00:03:39.830
John, I will see you on Friday.

